.TH KISS 3
.SH NAME
kiss \- a packet device over a byte stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bind '#k' /net/kiss
.PP
.B /net/kiss/kiss0/ctl
.br
.B /net/kiss/kiss0/data
.br
.B /net/kiss/kiss0/stats
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I kiss
device puts a message boundary on a byte stream, so that what is on the
other side of a serial line can be a packet device.
.PP
A TNC speaks KISS, and a KISS data frame carries exactly what
.I ax25 (3)
builds: the address field, the control byte, the PID and the
information, with no flags, no bit stuffing and no frame check
sequence, because the TNC puts those on the air itself.  So this is the
shortest path from this tree to a real amateur station.
.B #a
attaches to a file descriptor,
.B #k
is one, and the hardware beyond the serial line does the HDLC and the
modulation that
.I ax25 (3)
says it does not do.
.PP
It is also the only honest way to drive the link layers with no radio.
.B doc/radio-interface.ms
records that
.I lora (3)
could not be run over a
.IR pipe (2)
because a pipe is a byte stream and keeps no message boundary.  KISS is
precisely the framing that was missing, so a pipe with this on one end
is a packet device and the whole stack above runs in one kernel with
nothing else fitted.
.PP
The framing is SLIP's, from the KISS specification:
.PP
.EX
	FEND  0xc0	frame delimiter
	FESC  0xdb	escape
	TFEND 0xdc	FEND, escaped
	TFESC 0xdd	FESC, escaped
.EE
.PP
A frame is FEND, a type byte, the data, FEND, with FEND and FESC
escaped inside.  The type byte's low four bits are the command and the
high four the TNC port; 0 is a data frame and the rest configure the
TNC.  Nothing is acknowledged and nothing is retried: KISS puts a
boundary on a byte stream and claims to be nothing else.
.PP
.B Ctl
takes:
.TP
.BI fd " n"
Attach the byte stream open on file descriptor
.IR n .
The device keeps its own reference, so the process that wrote this may
exit.  Once only.
.TP
.BI txdelay " n"
.TP
.BI persist " n"
.TP
.BI slottime " n"
.TP
.BI txtail " n"
.TP
.BI duplex " on\fPor\fB off"
Sent to the TNC as command frames.  What they mean is the TNC's
business \- txdelay and txtail are tens of milliseconds of transmitter
keying, persist and slottime its channel access \- and nothing here
interprets them.  A TNC that ignores one says nothing about it.
.PP
.B Data
is one frame per read and one per write, and inherits
.IR radio (3)'s
refusals: a read whose buffer is smaller than the frame fails and the
frame waits, and a write past the frame size fails naming it.
.PP
The
.B iounit
is 272.  AX.25 2.2's
.B N1
defaults to 256 bytes of information, and an address field with two
digipeaters, a control byte and a PID is 30 more, so 272 is the number
at which a conversation on
.I ax25 (3)
lands on the specification's own default with no path and a little
under it with one.  A TNC's own buffer is usually larger; this is the
promise this device makes and not the far end's.
.PP
A frame that is not a data frame is counted under
.B notdata
and dropped, because a TNC sends those back on some hardware and they
are not packets.  A FESC followed by neither TFEND nor TFESC drops the
frame rather than guessing: a byte taken as data there is a byte in the
middle of somebody's address field.
.SH SOURCE
.B /os/inferno/radio/devkiss.c
.SH BUGS
One interface, which is a constant, and one TNC port: the type byte's
high four bits are written as zero and are not looked at on the way in.
.PP
Nothing asks the byte stream for an
.BR iounit ,
unlike
.I lora (3)
and
.I ax25 (3)
of what is under them.  What is under this is a byte stream, which
carries no messages and has no size to publish.
.PP
This has not been run against a TNC.  The framing is checked in both
directions against
.IR kisschk (1),
which assembles and reads the bytes from the specification, and the
whole of
.I ax25 (3)
and
.I aprs (1)
runs over it through a pipe; what has not happened is a serial line
with hardware on the end of it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.I kisschk (1),
.I ax25 (3),
.I aprs (1),
.I radio (3)
